P18-V01 request-future scheduler runtime-clock isolation:
- A full Producer suite run exposed that one process-wide Tokio task could be
  attached to another test/runtime's paused clock and expire futures too early.
  The timeout scheduler now tracks one shared-table scan per Tokio runtime ID;
  every scan expires entries registered in that runtime only. Request
  preparation ensures a scanner exists on its current runtime.
- Added a deterministic regression using two paused current-thread Tokio
  runtimes: advancing runtime B does not expire runtime A's future, and
  advancing runtime A beyond the timeout does. Correlation assertions remain
  key-scoped because the table is process-wide and other tests run concurrently.
- `cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-producer --all-features --locked` — PASS
  (142 Producer unit tests and all Producer integration/doc targets).
- `cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-core -p rocketmq-classic-producer
  -p rocketmq-classic --all-features --locked` — PASS (164 Core unit tests,
  142 Producer unit tests, facade API tests, and all selected integration/doc
  targets).
- `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --all-features --locked -- -D
  warnings`, `cargo fmt --all -- --check`, and `git diff --check` — PASS.
- This closes the runtime clock-isolation issue only. The full workspace
  current-tree P18-V02 rerun, broad P18-V01 audit, current GitHub Ubuntu/P18-V04
  evidence, Phase 19 Docker/Broker automation, and version/registry resolution
  remain open. No Docker or live Broker was run.
